From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nabeel Sowan <nabeel@vibes.se>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 16:13:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Leave a copied section's output offset at zero

setup_sections() set each input section's output_offset to the
section's address. The output offset is the input section's position
within its output section, which is the same section here, so it is
zero. bfd adds the output section's address when it writes the symbol
table, so the address was counted twice. Every .symtab value in a file
libr rewrote was wrong, most of them exactly double. A debugger then
cannot name the function it is stopped in:

    (gdb) info symbol $pc
    No symbol matches $pc.

The dynamic symbol table is not affected, so linking and loading were
never broken. That is why this has gone unseen. Debug symbols and
backtraces are what suffer.

Set it to zero, as objcopy does. Tested as for the previous change: a
rewritten file's .symtab, .dynsym, sections and program headers match
the input, and gdb resolves main in it.
---
 src/libr-bfd.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/libr-bfd.c b/src/libr-bfd.c
index f4e46b9..8a47e14 100644
--- a/src/libr-bfd.c
+++ b/src/libr-bfd.c
@@ -233,7 +233,11 @@ int setup_sections(bfd *ihandle, bfd *ohandle)
 		}
 		oscn->entsize = iscn->entsize;
 		iscn->output_section = oscn;
-		iscn->output_offset = vma;
+		/* The offset of the input section within its output section,
+		 * which is the same section here: zero, as objcopy has it. The
+		 * section's address was being added a second time, so every
+		 * .symtab value in a rewritten file was wrong. */
+		iscn->output_offset = 0;
 		if(
 			#ifdef HAVE_BFD_2_45
 			!bfd_copy_private_section_data(ihandle, iscn, ohandle, oscn, NULL)
